   "answer": "The anchored deviation estimator used by Task-CoEvolve is defined as \\(\\hat{S}(h) = \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{t \\in T} \\bar{p}_t + \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{t \\in S_k} \\frac{x_t(h) - \\bar{p}_t}{\\pi_t}\\), where \\(\\bar{p}_t\\) is the historical success rate (anchor) for task \\(t\\), \\(x_t(h)\\) is the observed outcome, and \\(\\pi_t\\) is the sampling probability [3:15]. The raw outcome is not weighted directly because using \\(x_t(h)/\\pi_t\\) alone would cause instability when tasks have extreme historical success rates (near 0 or 1) and small \\(\\pi_t\\), leading to dominant terms that distort estimation; instead, weighting the deviation \\(x_t(h) - \\bar{p}_t\\) ensures the anchor cancels in expectation and provides stable, comparable estimates across iterations [3:15]. This avoids the issue where a consistently solved task with tiny \\(\\pi_t\\) would otherwise dominate Equation (3) [3:15]."
  },
